PHP EMITTED ONLY CLASSES. Three of four type containers had no branch in
the symbol walk, so an in-file `interface` surfaced as an
`external_symbol` and members of an interface or trait were emitted BARE
(`area`, not `Shape.area`) because the enclosing-owner walk recognised
`class_declaration` alone — while the sibling _enclosing_type_name helper
already walked all four. Fixed with ONE branch keyed off a
container->kind map rather than three copies of the class branch, so a
fifth container cannot be added to _PHP_TYPE_CONTAINERS and silently
skipped.

All three of PHP's stacked defects fall out of that one change: the
interface becomes a real node, its member is owner-qualified, and the
relationship self-corrects from extends->external_symbol to
implements->interface — because the inheritance linker classifies by the
TARGET's kind, which is why Java gets this right despite lumping extends
and implements into one base_classes meta. PHP now emits
dispatches_to: Shape.area -> Square.area; its parity cell is promoted
from hole to hard pass in the same PR.

TYPESCRIPT records `abstract` on abstract classes. Five languages mark it
in modifiers, which is where is_abstract_type reads it; TS emitted an
empty list. Nothing had to be inferred — the grammar hands us a distinct
abstract_class_declaration node and the Symbol never set the field.

C++ EMITS PURE VIRTUALS, and a class declaring one is marked abstract.
The member walk filtered out every function declaration, so
`virtual int area() = 0;` vanished and an abstract base had no method to
dispatch FROM. Scoped to pure virtuals deliberately: a non-pure
declaration has its definition in another translation unit and there is
no decl/def merging for cpp, so emitting those would yield two symbols
for one method. A pure virtual has no definition anywhere by
construction. Guarded by a test asserting non-pure decls stay skipped.

C++'s cell STAYS RED on a different cause. Every ingredient is now
present and no edge appears, because _extends_admits_dispatch asks
whether the CHILD is abstract while a C++ abstract-base hierarchy has the
abstract PARENT (extends: Square -> Shape, Square concrete). The rule is
correct for the case it was written for (Go embedding, C# interface
inheritance — both endpoints abstract, so the tests coincide) and wrong
for abstract-base inheritance. Widening it changes dead-code reachability
across all four NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS languages, so it is filed as WI-nizug
rather than bundled here.

Two defensive branches in the pure-virtual detector carry pragmas with
reasons: `virtual T f() = 1;` and a virtual data member are both invalid
C++, so no well-formed input reaches them.

